WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.703
Hey, good morning, Ziad.

00:00:01.703 --> 00:00:03.902
Hope last night was a good one.

00:00:03.902 --> 00:00:06.598
It's Saturday, November twenty-eighth.

00:00:06.598 --> 00:00:11.068
Easy day on the calendar, mostly a run and then nothing at all.

00:00:11.068 --> 00:00:13.693
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:13.693 --> 00:00:21.568
What's the name for the simplest kind of learning, where you just stop noticing something that keeps happening?

00:00:21.568 --> 00:00:22.987
Answer's at the end.

00:00:22.987 --> 00:00:23.980
Okay, weather.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:33.062
Dry, gray, and around fifty, but with a west wind of fifteen to twenty-five, so the run and any ride will feel colder than that.

00:00:33.062 --> 00:00:41.718
Sunday's dry too, then heavy rain and gusts near forty roll in overnight into Monday morning, so plan for a soggy commute.

00:00:41.718 --> 00:00:42.711
Okay, the day.

00:00:42.711 --> 00:00:46.684
Calendar has your overnight plans wrapping up at eleven.

00:00:46.684 --> 00:00:54.914
Then right at eleven there's the interval run on there, five rounds of four minutes hard, about fifty minutes total.

00:00:54.914 --> 00:00:56.333
After that, nothing.

00:00:56.333 --> 00:00:59.242
The whole afternoon and evening are open.

00:00:59.242 --> 00:01:00.235
Alright, news.

00:01:00.235 --> 00:01:01.299
Just two today.

00:01:01.299 --> 00:01:04.279
OpenAI and Apple are now suing each other.

00:01:04.279 --> 00:01:11.729
Back in July, Apple sued over a former employee who apparently walked off with more than a hundred files.

00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:19.249
On Friday OpenAI countersued in California, saying Apple's the one playing dirty, by leaning on suppliers.

00:01:19.249 --> 00:01:23.861
A trial's set for July of next year, so this one's going to drag.

00:01:23.861 --> 00:01:26.060
And kind of a Black Friday one.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:26.770
Those A.I.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.175
agents that shop for you are running into walls.

00:01:30.175 --> 00:01:32.871
Amazon is blocking them from its site.

00:01:32.871 --> 00:01:38.760
Meanwhile Shopify says sales coming in through agents are up more than three times.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:44.365
But in Visa's own tests, the agents failed at about four in ten shopping tasks.

00:01:44.365 --> 00:01:48.834
So, apparently, not quite ready to buy your gifts unsupervised.

00:01:48.834 --> 00:01:50.821
Okay, here's the main thing.

00:01:50.821 --> 00:02:01.108
Bark Barbecue, the Dominican-style barbecue spot, opened a big place on Thames Street this summer, and it's an easy one to forget is right there.

00:02:01.108 --> 00:02:04.940
It's Texas-style smoking mixed with Dominican cooking.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:15.014
Fourteen-hour brisket, longaniza sausage, a chicharron sandwich that apparently made a New York Times list of sandwiches that define the city.

00:02:15.014 --> 00:02:18.916
Sides like maduros and an oregano-heavy mac and cheese.

00:02:18.916 --> 00:02:21.825
The part you'd probably like is the room.

00:02:21.825 --> 00:02:28.211
There are four smokers behind a glass wall, and they slice the meat right in front of you.

00:02:28.211 --> 00:02:32.042
Big communal tables, an orange-tiled bar, and a patio.

00:02:32.042 --> 00:02:38.214
Oh, and it opens at nine for coffee, with breakfast tacos on homemade tallow tortillas.

00:02:38.214 --> 00:02:43.110
So it could be a slow Sunday morning thing tomorrow, before the rain.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.309
Or a dinner sometime next week.

00:02:45.309 --> 00:02:49.708
Hours were from the summer, so they might've shifted a little.

00:02:49.708 --> 00:02:52.120
Today's challenge is a social one.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:57.867
Pick one person you've been meaning to see and invite them to something specific.

00:02:57.867 --> 00:03:01.698
Not "we should hang out", but an actual day and place.

00:03:01.698 --> 00:03:03.401
The barbecue spot works.

00:03:03.401 --> 00:03:09.573
Most plans die at the vague stage, and a specific invite is weirdly easy to say yes to.

00:03:09.573 --> 00:03:11.347
Okay, something to learn.

00:03:11.347 --> 00:03:16.881
You spend all day shooting silver, so here's what's actually going on with it.

00:03:16.881 --> 00:03:23.479
Sterling silver is about ninety-two and a half percent silver, and the rest is mostly copper.

00:03:23.479 --> 00:03:27.027
That's why you see nine-twenty-five stamped on it.

00:03:27.027 --> 00:03:30.290
Pure silver is too soft to make a fork out of.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.064
It would bend in a steak.

00:03:32.064 --> 00:03:33.909
The copper stiffens it up.

00:03:33.909 --> 00:03:36.108
Silver plate is something else.

00:03:36.108 --> 00:03:44.906
It's a cheaper base metal, like brass or nickel, with a really thin coat of silver put on electrically, thinner than a hair.

00:03:44.906 --> 00:03:46.466
It looks the same new.

00:03:46.466 --> 00:03:57.038
But on old pieces you can sometimes see the plate worn through, usually on the back of a spoon or the tips of fork tines, where it touches the table.

00:03:57.038 --> 00:03:57.889
Now tarnish.

00:03:57.889 --> 00:04:02.004
Most people think it's rust, like oxygen eating the metal.

00:04:02.004 --> 00:04:03.210
It's not, really.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.552
Silver barely cares about oxygen.

00:04:05.552 --> 00:04:07.680
What it reacts with is sulfur.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:16.194
There are tiny amounts of sulfur gases in the air, plus more from eggs, onions, wool, rubber bands, even some cardboard.

00:04:16.194 --> 00:04:22.437
The sulfur bonds with the silver on the surface and makes silver sulfide, which is dark.

00:04:22.437 --> 00:04:23.998
Humidity speeds it up.

00:04:23.998 --> 00:04:25.630
And here's a neat part.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:31.022
Tarnish goes yellow, then brown, then kind of blue and purple, before black.

00:04:31.022 --> 00:04:33.434
Those early colors aren't pigment.

00:04:33.434 --> 00:04:40.529
The film is so thin that light bouncing off the top and bottom of it interferes, like a soap bubble.

00:04:40.529 --> 00:04:47.979
That's why lightly tarnished silver can throw a warm cast in a photo even when it doesn't look dirty yet.

00:04:47.979 --> 00:04:49.894
The fix is kind of fun too.

00:04:49.894 --> 00:04:55.144
Polish works by scrubbing off a tiny bit of silver along with the tarnish.

00:04:55.144 --> 00:05:00.040
But hot water, baking soda, and aluminum foil does it with chemistry.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:08.057
The sulfur jumps from the silver over to the aluminum, and the silver underneath goes back to being plain silver.

00:05:08.057 --> 00:05:09.121
Nothing's lost.

00:05:09.121 --> 00:05:14.868
So: calendar says plans till eleven, then the interval run, then a wide-open day.

00:05:14.868 --> 00:05:18.415
And try making one real plan with one real person.

00:05:18.415 --> 00:05:19.763
Oh, and the answer.

00:05:19.763 --> 00:05:25.084
It's habituation, like a reminder left in plain sight that you stop seeing.

00:05:25.084 --> 00:05:27.000
Have a good Saturday, Ziad.
